4
00:00:49,461 --> 00:00:51,590
Earlier, what have we learned?

5
00:00:51,590 --> 00:01:06,317
We started our discussion on tracking. We learned the basic idea of what are the different things that we can track. Broadly, three things we can track: one is the user's sense organs,

6
00:01:06,317 --> 00:01:17,610
the other one is other body parts, the user's other body parts, and the third thing that we talked about which we can track is the external or surrounding environment of the

7
00:01:17,610 --> 00:01:24,524
user's physical environment, that is the objects that are present in the physical environment.

8
00:01:24,524 --> 00:01:28,749
Then we briefly covered the tracking process.

9
00:01:29,069 --> 00:01:29,790
How do we track?

10
00:01:30,951 --> 00:01:33,314
It has three distinct components.

11
00:01:33,334 --> 00:01:37,739
One is trying to understand how the trackers work.

12
00:01:37,759 --> 00:01:39,040
So, broadly there are two types.

13
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:41,403
One is two types of trackers.

14
00:01:41,943 --> 00:01:44,867
One is inside-out, the other one is outside-in.

15
00:01:46,408 --> 00:01:54,177
In inside-out, we have all the sensors placed on the device that the user is using.

16
00:01:54,832 --> 00:02:00,537
In outside-in, we have sensors placed in the surrounding environment rather than on the device that the user is using.

17
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:10,485
Both have their pros and cons that we have briefly touched on.

18
00:02:11,928 --> 00:02:16,091
Also, we started understanding the process, how it works.

19
00:02:17,473 --> 00:02:21,376
There are broadly four stages in the overall process.

20
00:02:22,397 --> 00:02:23,218
Calibration.

21
00:02:23,722 --> 00:02:27,566
registration, integration and drift correction.

22
00:02:28,007 --> 00:02:30,590
So, we got a brief idea of each of these stages.

23
00:02:30,690 --> 00:02:34,334
Let us go into a little bit more detail on those stages.

24
00:02:35,415 --> 00:02:40,060
So, we are going to continue our discussion on these four stages in this lecture.

25
00:02:43,684 --> 00:02:51,433
Now, in order to further understand each of those stages, let us take a use case of a particular type of sensor.

26
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:54,583
That is the IMU sensors.

27
00:02:54,924 --> 00:03:08,821
So here what we will do is although we are using IMU sensors as an example to explain the process in a little bit more detail, the idea remains the same for all other types of sensors.

28
00:03:11,344 --> 00:03:18,973
So this is purely for simplicity rather than a discussion on specific tracking techniques.

29
00:03:21,603 --> 00:03:26,313
So, before we start, let us have a quick understanding of what the IMU sensors are.

30
00:03:26,313 --> 00:03:50,175
Now, IMU stands for Inertial Measurement Unit, I, M, and U. This is a broad and quite popular group of sensors, a composite hardware containing

31
00:03:50,577 --> 00:04:04,054
three types of sensors mainly, which are nowadays mostly part of many HMDs or head-mounted displays.

32
00:04:06,077 --> 00:04:12,625
One of those sensors is the gyroscope, then accelerometer and finally magnetometer.

33
00:04:12,925 --> 00:04:18,913
So, we have gyroscope which captures the angular velocity, that is gyroscope.

34
00:04:19,517 --> 00:04:28,130
Then, in case of an accelerometer, it captures the linear acceleration along the three axes that is x, y, and z axis.

35
00:04:29,171 --> 00:04:34,839
And finally, magnetometer which captures magnetic field strength along the three axes.

36
00:04:36,301 --> 00:04:46,335
So, when we are using the term IMU sensor, we are referring to these sensors, these three sensors together in a composite form as a single piece of hardware.

37
00:04:47,007 --> 00:04:55,417
which are typically part of many modern-day devices including your mobile phones as well as many of the HMDs.

38
00:05:01,344 --> 00:05:09,994
Now, when I say that we are using IMU sensor for tracking, it may be noted that tracking

39
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:24,456
with IMU sensor relies heavily or mainly on the gyroscopic readings that is the values that are coming from the gyroscope which is primarily angular velocity.

40
00:05:26,959 --> 00:05:39,593
Other sensors, namely the accelerometer and the magnetometer. These two sensors are not directly contributing to the tracking estimation, but

41
00:05:40,619 --> 00:05:47,929
We can use them to detect drift errors and take corrective measures.

42
00:05:54,258 --> 00:05:58,864
Now let us try to understand each of those steps assuming IMU sensors.

43
00:05:59,325 --> 00:06:02,590
Let us start with the first stage that is calibration.

44
00:06:02,610 --> 00:06:04,052
So what happens in calibration?

45
00:06:06,395 --> 00:06:10,120
Now the gyroscope gives us angular velocity.

46
00:06:12,293 --> 00:06:24,629
Now, this angular velocity is captured in the form of some numbers, but this is not the exact angular velocity that ideally should be there in a perfect world.

47
00:06:28,595 --> 00:06:40,090
So, measured angular velocity of the gyroscope, let us call it omega g, and the actual angular velocity, let us call it omega a,

48
00:06:41,167 --> 00:06:42,890
are likely to be different.

49
00:06:43,451 --> 00:06:46,316
We are assuming that our sensors are not perfect.

50
00:06:46,336 --> 00:06:48,098
So, they are likely to be different.

51
00:06:48,119 --> 00:06:55,451
Now, due to imperfect devices and the measurement technique that we are adopting, the difference comes.

52
00:06:55,972 --> 00:07:07,731
So, the measurement error in this case is omega g the measured velocity versus omega a the actual velocity.

53
00:07:08,200 --> 00:07:18,253
So, omega G minus omega A is the measurement error which is quite obvious.

54
00:07:18,273 --> 00:07:19,915
Now, how do we relate the two values?

55
00:07:19,975 --> 00:07:28,186
So, our objective is to basically in the calibration stage, our objective is to make these two values as close to each other as possible.

56
00:07:30,209 --> 00:07:34,815
Now, one way to relate both can be a linear relationship.

57
00:07:35,335 --> 00:07:37,258
Through a linear relationship.

58
00:07:37,458 --> 00:07:48,353
Say, we can propose a relationship like omega g is a plus b into omega a. This is a linear relationship.

59
00:07:49,214 --> 00:07:57,846
Now, when omega g is equal to omega a, we can say that a is 0 and b is 1.

60
00:07:58,147 --> 00:08:02,052
So, if we replace the value of a with 0 and b with 1,

61
00:08:02,707 --> 00:08:05,351
then we can see that both become equal.

62
00:08:05,752 --> 00:08:13,063
So, that is the perfect condition, ideal condition that we want to be in, but that never happens in practice.

63
00:08:13,083 --> 00:08:19,292
But if we have this type of relationship, note that this is not the only way to relate the two.

64
00:08:19,412 --> 00:08:29,828
Here, we are just for the sake of simplicity, we are assuming there is a linear relationship between these two values and we have to estimate the

65
00:08:30,466 --> 00:08:32,648
parameters here, a, b, and so on.

66
00:08:35,712 --> 00:08:54,313
So, assuming this relationship holds, we can have the measurement error represented as omega g minus omega a equals a plus b omega a minus omega a. So, we are replacing omega g with this expression

67
00:08:59,828 --> 00:09:14,106
And we can reformulate it to be A plus B minus 1 times omega A. So, this is the expression for measurement error.

68
00:09:14,126 --> 00:09:26,983
Next, what we can do to calibrate, that means to reduce or make this measurement error as minimal as possible, what can we do?

69
00:09:28,027 --> 00:09:40,559
That involves taking many samples, sometimes thousands, from cheap and inaccurate sensors, which we are calling omega g, that is the measurement.

70
00:09:40,579 --> 00:09:52,511
We are assuming that our sensor that we have at our disposal is cheap and inaccurate, and also expensive and highly accurate sensors WA.

71
00:09:53,072 --> 00:09:56,295
Note here that when I say expensive or highly accurate,

72
00:09:56,782 --> 00:10:04,213
that can still be not the exact or ideal scenario, but it may be very close.

73
00:10:04,774 --> 00:10:20,697
So, the sensor that we are using to represent the real value is supposed to be highly accurate as compared to the inexpensive or cheap ones or inaccurate ones that we are assuming we have.

74
00:10:23,478 --> 00:10:31,449
So, we are assuming for calibration that we have access to thousands of samples of data from two types of sensors.

75
00:10:31,469 --> 00:10:44,198
One is the IMU sensors which is assumed to be cheap or inaccurate, vis-a-vis a highly accurate one, benchmark data set which we are calling omega A,

76
00:10:44,198 --> 00:10:51,797
and then we can compare these values. Then one way to compare is

77
00:10:52,705 --> 00:10:56,429
A very simple approach is to compute the sum of squared errors.

78
00:10:57,770 --> 00:11:02,355
So this is the expression; this is the difference.

79
00:11:02,375 --> 00:11:17,811
We are squaring it and then taking the sum of all such squared values, that is for all samples, for all pairs of samples.

80
00:11:17,831 --> 00:11:22,155
So we have n values

81
00:11:22,827 --> 00:11:32,237
for omega A and n values for omega G. For each pair, we are computing the difference, then taking the square, and then summing up all those values.

82
00:11:36,622 --> 00:11:51,518
This is a simple or straightforward optimization problem where we are trying to find out constraints, constants C1 and C2, which optimize the expression

83
00:11:52,595 --> 00:11:55,659
C1 plus C2 WG minus WA.

84
00:12:00,004 --> 00:12:02,547
This is a classical regression problem.

85
00:12:07,494 --> 00:12:13,121
So, the square term of C1 plus C2 WG minus WA, this we are trying to optimize.

86
00:12:14,583 --> 00:12:18,147
This is a classical regression problem, which is referred to as

87
00:12:18,413 --> 00:12:19,975
linear least squares.

88
00:12:20,356 --> 00:12:23,280
This is only one of the many techniques that are there.

89
00:12:25,844 --> 00:12:38,082
Again, we are referring to these simple techniques for simplicity, for the sake of simplicity.

90
00:12:38,102 --> 00:12:45,973
Now, once we are able to determine C1 and C2, then we can

91
00:12:46,628 --> 00:12:56,635
transform the cheap sensor reading in the following way: calibrated value omega cal can be C1 plus C2 omega g.

92
00:12:56,635 --> 00:13:08,977
So, this essentially maps the cheap sensor output to the expensive sensor output, or in other words, to the closest match possible with the real value.

93
00:13:10,459 --> 00:13:15,505
But here we are doing it mathematically rather than using an actually very expensive or

94
00:13:15,772 --> 00:13:17,394
very accurate sensor.

95
00:13:18,335 --> 00:13:27,945
So, this stage where we are mathematically correcting what we are getting to what ideally we should get is called calibration.

96
00:13:28,326 --> 00:13:33,011
So, I hope this is a better way of understanding things rather than going through the definition.

97
00:13:34,673 --> 00:13:44,023
To summarize calibration, what we are doing based on our datasets is finding out the C1 and C2 constants in this case.

98
00:13:44,948 --> 00:13:52,716
In a simple case, if we are using any other relationship, the constants may be different in number and form.

99
00:13:53,917 --> 00:14:04,081
However, the idea is to find out those constants and then use those constants to formulate an expression to transform or to map the actual sensor value that we are

100
00:14:04,081 --> 00:14:14,719
getting to a value that is close to the ideal ones through equations such as this, that is calibration.

101
00:14:18,091 --> 00:14:21,535
Next, let us try to understand registration.

102
00:14:23,237 --> 00:14:33,428
Now this is basically finding out the initial pose, initial orientation of the sensor or in other words the user who is using the sensor.

103
00:14:34,930 --> 00:14:41,938
So through this step we are setting initial alignment between the real and virtual worlds.

104
00:14:43,454 --> 00:14:50,744
Symbolically, it is referred to as the alignment represented by the symbol theta at the instance t equal to 0.

105
00:14:54,970 --> 00:15:08,649
We can think of it as whichever direction the headset is facing when the tracking system is turned on that will be the initial orientation and setting initial alignment to be the forward direction.

106
00:15:08,769 --> 00:15:13,275
These are some of the simplifying assumptions that we can make to understand the process.

107
00:15:16,192 --> 00:15:28,365
In order to do that, in order to do the registration stage, what we need are fiducial markers or reflectors or tags which can aid in the registration significantly.

108
00:15:29,066 --> 00:15:32,309
Even if they are not present, through computer vision techniques, it can be done.

109
00:15:32,730 --> 00:15:35,973
But if they are present, then the process becomes simpler.

110
00:15:37,795 --> 00:15:40,258
Then the third stage is integration.

111
00:15:43,782 --> 00:15:54,335
The sensory output, the sensor that we are using, the output of that sensor, the data stream usually arrives at a regular sampling rate, regular interval, discrete interval.

112
00:15:57,699 --> 00:16:12,557
Now, orientation of the sensor at the time instance t is represented as theta t, which can be expressed as k into delta t.

113
00:16:12,655 --> 00:16:17,842
where delta t is the sampling interval that can be computed.

114
00:16:18,242 --> 00:16:29,156
So, our objective is to compute the orientation or pose after some time from the initial orientation and pose that we have estimated in the registration phase.

115
00:16:30,397 --> 00:16:38,848
Now, the time lapse is k delta t where delta t is the sampling interval and k is an integer number.

116
00:16:41,191 --> 00:16:47,661
Now, this orientation can be computed by integrating the samples.

117
00:16:49,343 --> 00:16:51,847
Equation wise, we can represent it in this way.

118
00:16:51,887 --> 00:17:08,311
Theta t or the orientation at time t equals the initial orientation theta 0 plus i equal to 1 to t. We are basically integrating this part W cal i dot

119
00:17:08,630 --> 00:17:12,973
delta t. So, what does this part mean?

120
00:17:14,236 --> 00:17:27,108
The idea is that the calibrated angular velocity sample at the ith instant which is W cal i is the angular velocity sample that we are getting from the gyroscopic sensor.

121
00:17:27,910 --> 00:17:36,679
This indicates rotation or in other words change in orientation by an amount W i cal delta t.

122
00:17:41,215 --> 00:17:53,011
And all such changes are summed up from the beginning to that time interval and then added to the initial orientation to get the

123
00:17:53,011 --> 00:18:06,187
current orientation at time instance t. That is the idea of integration.

124
00:18:06,748 --> 00:18:10,092
Next and the final part is drift correction.

125
00:18:12,434 --> 00:18:13,155
What happens here?

126
00:18:14,617 --> 00:18:27,972
Now, drift correction if you may recollect is basically compensating for the errors in estimation between the real and actual value.

127
00:18:28,753 --> 00:18:36,342
So, in order to understand that, note that this drift correction is usually done with the help of extra sensors.

128
00:18:37,143 --> 00:18:41,428
So, so far we are talking only about the gyroscope.

129
00:18:42,269 --> 00:18:47,055
But then in this case, we may be using other sensors such as cameras.

130
00:18:49,018 --> 00:18:57,429
In the case of IMU, we can use accelerometer and magnetometer readings for drift correction in addition to the gyroscope values.

131
00:18:58,671 --> 00:19:03,838
But in general, drift correction requires the use of other sensors.

132
00:19:10,518 --> 00:19:20,690
So, we have at least two sensors and we need to blend their values to estimate the drift and correct it.

133
00:19:20,711 --> 00:19:36,350
A classic approach to blend the two sensors, say in our IMU sensor example gyroscope and there may be a camera present separately or maybe a magnetometer or maybe an accelerometer.

134
00:19:38,092 --> 00:19:39,073
We can say that

135
00:19:40,336 --> 00:19:56,739
theta dk equals alpha times theta gk plus 1 minus alpha times theta ck, where theta dk this term is the drift-corrected orientation at time instant t.

136
00:19:56,739 --> 00:20:09,938
Then theta gk this term is the orientation obtained by integrating gyroscope values at the kth instant.

137
00:20:10,846 --> 00:20:22,638
through that integration phase, theta ck, this term, is the orientation obtained with camera at kth instant.

138
00:20:22,698 --> 00:20:25,041
So, we are having two different sensors.

139
00:20:26,262 --> 00:20:34,571
We are applying integration to each of those sensors and we are obtaining the corresponding orientations for the two sensors separately.

140
00:20:35,332 --> 00:20:40,397
And then we are combining them through this expression as shown here.

141
00:20:42,115 --> 00:20:55,692
Now, alpha in this particular expression classical approach the term alpha is a normalized value between 0 to 1 it is called the gain parameter.

142
00:20:57,655 --> 00:21:03,162
So, using the gain parameter we can combine the two individually computed orientations

143
00:21:03,985 --> 00:21:17,558
at time interval at the time instance t and then we can get the drift corrected orientation at the same instant by combining them using the gain parameter alpha.

144
00:21:23,184 --> 00:21:26,807
So, that is in summary how the process works.

145
00:21:29,630 --> 00:21:30,651
Just to recollect,

146
00:21:31,255 --> 00:21:41,009
we have gone a little bit deeper into the four stages namely calibration, registration, integration and drift correction.

147
00:21:41,730 --> 00:21:53,848
So, in calibration we have seen how we can estimate or how we can get the sensory values as close as possible.

148
00:21:53,888 --> 00:22:00,638
The idea of calibration is we do not want to use highly accurate sensors which are generally costly.

149
00:22:01,867 --> 00:22:10,571
Instead, we will be using cheap sensors, but through the calibration stage, we will be transforming the values of those cheap sensors to

150
00:22:10,571 --> 00:22:16,927
the values of highly accurate sensors through some mathematical manipulation.

151
00:22:17,748 --> 00:22:28,802
And to determine that mathematical expression to transform, we need to have access to a set of datasets, to a set of data samples,

152
00:22:29,744 --> 00:22:37,812
from both the sensors with which we are calibrating or what is our underlying assumption about accurate sensors.

153
00:22:39,814 --> 00:22:46,790
Once the calibration is done, then our sensor is ready to collect data. Then the next thing we should do is basically

154
00:22:46,790 --> 00:22:58,013
assuming the current position of the sensor and the orientation of the user, we can set the

155
00:22:58,550 --> 00:23:08,185
initial position and orientation of the tracked body parts or objects as per the need that is the registration phase.

156
00:23:08,886 --> 00:23:23,722
After that, what we can do is we can keep on estimating the orientation at any time instant t.

157
00:23:23,722 --> 00:23:45,346
So, for that we require the initial orientation estimated in the registration phase and we sum up the sensory values to determine orientation at any arbitrary instant of time.

158
00:23:47,168 --> 00:23:50,612
In the drift correction phase, what we assume is that

159
00:23:51,723 --> 00:23:53,665
we have access to at least two sensors.

160
00:23:55,287 --> 00:24:11,908
The more the better and in this stage what we do is combine the orientation computed at the same time instant separately by the two sensors and then combine them together

161
00:24:12,580 --> 00:24:13,522
in various forms.

162
00:24:14,183 --> 00:24:16,266
There can be various forms of combinations.

163
00:24:16,486 --> 00:24:24,287
We have talked about one classical approach where we used a gain parameter, a normalized value between 0 to 1, to combine the

164
00:24:24,287 --> 00:24:30,709
separately estimated orientation at time instant t to get the drift-corrected orientation.

165
00:24:33,894 --> 00:24:36,438
So, with that, we have come to the end of this part of the lecture.

166
00:24:36,719 --> 00:24:39,263
We have covered

167
00:24:39,833 --> 00:24:48,646
a basic understanding of sensor types, sensor categories, and the sensor sensing process.

168
00:24:49,687 --> 00:24:56,337
What is left is the exact technology being used to sense; what are the different technologies that are there in the market.

169
00:24:58,600 --> 00:25:03,588
So, we will continue our discussion on sensing technologies in the next part of the lecture.

170
00:25:04,890 --> 00:25:05,250
I hope

171
00:25:06,108 --> 00:25:11,162
you understood the concepts clearly and enjoyed the discussion.

172
00:25:11,884 --> 00:25:14,009
Looking forward to meeting you in the next part.

